3" :question/id #uuid "ab0b47c0-5a92-5c1c-aef7-bc71e6c1d794" :question/math-academy-id "q-11193" :question/problem "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-3)^{n+1}}{{10}^{n}}$ for $n\\ge 1$ converge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{(-3)^{n+1}}{{10}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\cdot {(-3)}^{n}}{{10}^{n}} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{{(-3)}^{n}}{{10}^{n}} \\\\  & =-3{(-\\frac{3}{10})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now see that this is the geometric sequence with $r=-\\frac{3}{10}.$ \n\nSince $|r|<1,$ the sequence converges to $0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-11193/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c023b634-e106-5fbc-a8ff-197f3aa4869d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-11193"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-141314/selection/04807305688d046c901dfe9206d434cec6318beb3adc535ab23fa5189b949df5" :answer/id #uuid "f3b69a01-4ebc-54c8-bc14-99f4f4c13e16" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141314/selection/d047cfc48f7fb67c1aef6c4aa9b570461cd848e9a5e91649a7dfb5acb8d8c3de" :answer/id #uuid "e9854a4a-6ac4-58d5-a2f1-e31269baf73f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Converges to $8$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141314/selection/b47e51beb4c27a223e6b987d3fe8ec1111716a9c714e71b4c7194e50c65f6572" :answer/id #uuid "8a2eda53-ab