|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{{4}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2n}\\cdot x}{{4}^{n}} \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2n}}{{4}^{n}} \\\\  & =x\\cdot {(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.$ Since ${x}^{2}$ is positive, $r=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}$ is also positive.  So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for\n\n$$\n0<\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,0<{x}^{2}\\le 4.\n$$\n\nTaking into account that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n0<x\\le 2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-123344/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abad9fa9-b426-50aa-aaa7-d472ea974f98"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-123344"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-351812/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "e76fa6e3-84b4-5fe1-b544-d83aaff0d617" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351812/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "df8ee542-5787-5f0c-aa1d-64abe31b048a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-351812/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-351812/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-351812/field-2/f022eff7ecaa4435243255d76a2a932e99db9e71ca2b89b05fafca75800709a0" :answer/id #uuid "96fe9bdf-751c-5475-a4c1-20ac570cbf32" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\le" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351812/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "e918285d-b1e6-59a6-804c-73ea49b5420d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-351812/field-2/f022eff7ecaa4435243255d76a2a932e99db9e71ca2b89b05fafca75800709a0" :answer-field/choices ["q-351812/field-2/f022eff7ecaa4435243255d76a2a932e99db9e71ca2b89b05fafca75800709a0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-351812/field-3/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "89b5003b-d228-5664-b33b-fc76e3a0b964" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351812/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "7fe42bd9-1445-5ba6-a814-f64805c3ce4e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-351812/field-3/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-351812/field-3/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-351812" :question/id #uuid "5c54086d-c19a-5134-b16f-78dfd14ffbc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-351812" :question/problem "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{3n+2}}{{x}^{2n}},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ Find the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges.\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$<x$\n\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{x}^{3n+2}}{{x}^{2n}} \\\\  & ={x}^{\\,3n+2-2n} \\\\  & ={x}^{\\,n+2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=x.$ Since $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for\n\n$$\n0<x\\le 1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-351812/field-1" "q-351812/field-2" "q-351812/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abad9fa9-b426-50aa-aaa7-d472ea974f98"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-351812"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-647"] :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r| <           1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r| > 1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $a_1$\n\n- if $r = -1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form $a_n = a \\cdot r^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-3)^{n}}{{5}^{n+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-3)^{n}}{5\\cdot {5}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{(-3)^{n}}{{5}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now see that this is the geometric sequence with $r=-\\dfrac{3}{5} .$ \n\nSince $|r| < 1,$ the sequence converges to $0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-649"] :question/problem "Consider the geometric sequence \n\n$$\na_n =\\dfrac{x^2}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{16}{x^2}\\right)^{n}, \\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is a positive real number. For which values of $x$ is the sequence convergent?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r| <                      1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r| > 1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $a_1$\n\n- if $r = -1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r = \\dfrac{16}{x^2}.$ \nSince $x^2$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive, So, the convergence condition is $0 \\lt r\\leq 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0 \\lt\\dfrac{16}{x^2}\\leq 1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x^2 \\geq 16.\n$$\n\nTaking into account the fact that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\nx \\geq 4.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9990"] :question/problem "Consider the sequence $a_n = \\dfrac{x^{2n+1}}{x^{n-2}},$ where $x$ is a real positive number and $n\\geq 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r| <              1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r| > 1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $a_1$\n\n- if $r = -1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form $a_n = a \\cdot r^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{{x}^{n-2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2n+1-n+2} \\\\  & ={x}^{n+3} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with common ratio $r = x.$ Since $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is $0 \\lt  r \\leq 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0 \\lt  x \\leq 1 .\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
